417] [17592186508889 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508889 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508889 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508889 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d34519d1-654b-50eb-a652-7f98090b4427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508894 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508894 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508894 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508894 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9324986-dc8e-56cf-affb-81d9274ea958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508909 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508909 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508909 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508909 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fbadbf3f-b18a-5a99-9826-e9ff4c997442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508931 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5753a7d8-bd1a-52e7-a471-c1acec6a4f75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508931 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508936 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508936 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508936 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508936 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508936 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508936 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508936 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fdf7d2e-21a5-5a00-afb9-716691a79836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508936 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =3-3{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3-3{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3-3{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =3x+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =3x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508968 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adce0112-6117-576f-afb6-900e0d57f067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508968 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\  & =16{e}^{-4x}(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}) \\\\  & =8{e}^{-4x}({e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =8-8{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8-8{e}^{-8x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8-8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =8x+{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =8x{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509005 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186509039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509005 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a815087-9d2f-5f79-be34-541c824a1cf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509005 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186508936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509015 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186509005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509015 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ce6f69a-e8bd-5bb3-8b7f-545dd51be273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509015 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509019 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186509020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509019 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8202d26a-ddb4-51a5-a248-916138bbdab9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509019 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186508931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509020 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8d89a719-5776-5738-90c1-4252105b7daa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509020 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186397752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509039 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186509019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509039 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d34432c1-07b0-58c4-a35d-f354ab6b00c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509039 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186508968 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
